<!-- §01 — The Deployment Gap -->
<section id="s-gap" data-code="gap">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Deployment Gap</div>
<h2>Models are trained one way, deployed another</h2>
<p>
  Standard reinforcement learning for LLMs — RLHF, RLVR, GRPO — optimizes a single objective:
  given a problem <em>x</em>, produce a response <em>y</em> that maximizes reward <em>r*(x, y)</em>.
  One question. One answer. One gradient step.
</p>
<p>
  But real-world deployments don't stop there. Code assistants run programs, catch errors, and retry.
  Math solvers attempt a proof, check it, and revise. Planning agents try a schedule, get feedback on
  cost constraints, and iterate. These systems — sequential revision, MCTS, Mind Evolution — all share
  a single primitive: given a previous attempt and feedback, produce an improved response.
</p>
<p>
  This creates a fundamental mismatch. A model optimized for JOneShot may still struggle at JφSR:
  two policies can be indistinguishable under single-shot metrics, yet one might be dramatically
  better at multi-step revision (Theorem 3.2). Training on one-shot rewards simply doesn't teach
  the skill that deployment actually needs.
</p>

