  There exist policies that are identical under JOneShot but differ substantially under JφSR. Training only on JOneShot leaves the revision capability entirely to chance.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — The Credit Blur -->
<section id="s-credit" data-code="credit">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Credit Blur</div>
<h2>Multi-turn RL assigns the same credit to bad steps</h2>
<p>
  The natural fix for the deployment gap is multi-turn reinforcement learning: run sequential revision rollouts,
  observe the final reward, and backpropagate credit through all steps. But this creates a subtle and harmful problem.
</p>
<p>
  Consider a trajectory: <em>wrong answer y₁</em> → <em>wrong answer y₂</em> → <em>correct answer y₃</em>.
  The final reward is +1. Multi-turn RL propagates this reward backward through all three turns — including the
  two wrong ones. The gradient for "produce wrong answer y₁ from question x" receives the same positive signal
  as "produce correct answer y₃ from revision state."
</p>
<p>
  This credit is valid in expectation — across many rollouts, the biases cancel. But per-rollout, it is
  a noisy and coarse signal for teaching the model to revise well at each intermediate state.
  The model isn't learning "how to recover from mistake y₁" — it's learning "produce whatever led to
  eventual success," which is a much harder target to pin down.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">credit assignment comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click buttons to switch view</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="btn-multiturn">Multi-turn RL</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="btn-reves">REVES</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="credit-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p id="credit-caption" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0;text-align:center">
      Multi-turn RL: all steps receive the same +1 reward signal, including the wrong ones.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  REVES takes a different view: the wrong intermediate answers are not <em>actions to imitate</em>
  — they are <em>states from which the model should learn to recover</em>.
  Instead of assigning credit at the trajectory level, REVES asks a local question at each near-miss:
  <em>from this wrong answer, how likely is the model to produce a correct revision?</em>
  This one-step recovery probability is what REVES directly trains.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 — Near-Miss Recovery -->
<section id="s-recovery" data-code="recovery">
<div class="section-no">03 — Near-Miss Recovery</div>
<h2>Each near-miss is a standalone revision training task</h2>
<p>
  REVES builds on a clean mathematical decomposition (Lemma 4.1): the sequential revision objective
  JφSR factors exactly into a weighted sum of per-state one-step recovery probabilities along visited trajectories.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Lemma 4.1 — Hazard Decomposition</div>
  <span class="math-var">J</span><sup>SR</sup><sub>ϕ</sub>(<span class="math-var">θ</span>) = ∑<sub>z</sub> <span class="math-var">ρ</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">z</span>) · <span class="math-var">V</span><sub>π</sub>(<span class="math-var">z</span>)
  <br>
  <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.72rem">where z = (x, y<sub>prev</sub>, feedback) is a revision state, ρ<sub>θ</sub>(z) is how often z is visited, and V<sub>π</sub>(z) = Pr<sub>y'~π(·|z)</sub>[r*(x,y')=1] is the one-step recovery probability at z.</span>
</div>

<p>
  This decomposition reveals the algorithm: <strong>raise V(z) at any visited state z and JφSR goes up</strong>,
  without any horizon credit assignment. Each near-miss answer produces an independent, tractable
  training signal — simply sample fresh responses from the revision state and train on their outcomes.
</p>
<p>
  Concretely: if the model is stuck on "write a function to sort by second element of a tuple" and
  produces a wrong answer y₁, the revision state is z = (question, y₁, "test case 3 fails").
  Draw M fresh responses from πθ at z. Some succeed (green), some fail (red).
  V(z) = fraction that succeed. Train to raise this fraction.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">per-state recovery probability V(z)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">adjust branches M</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="btn-m3">M = 3</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="btn-m5">M = 5</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="btn-m10">M = 10</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="recovery-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p id="recovery-caption" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0;text-align:center">
      M=3 branches from revision state z — 1 succeeds (green), 2 fail (red) → V(z) ≈ 0.33
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The key insight is that the wrong intermediate answer is <em>not discarded or penalized</em> —
  it becomes a revision state that the model learns to escape from.
  This converts one successful long-horizon trajectory into several short-horizon single-turn revision problems,
  each with a clear and unambiguous training signal.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Theorem 3.2 — Objective Mismatch:</strong> For any revision length K ≥ 2, there exist policies π₁, π₂ and a problem distribution such that
  JOneShot(π₁) = JOneShot(π₂) while JφSR(π₂) − JφSR(π₁) ≥ Δ(K) > 0.
  Two policies indistinguishable on single-shot benchmarks can differ substantially on sequential revision.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Two-Stage Algorithm -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">04 — Two-Stage Algorithm</div>
<h2>Stage I generates near-misses; Stage II trains on them</h2>
<p>
  REVES realizes the per-state training signal with a two-stage iterative loop that alternates between
  data augmentation (Stage I) and policy optimization (Stage II).
  Critically, <strong>augmentation must be continual</strong>: once the model stops making earlier mistakes,
  static augmented data becomes uninformative. Each epoch refreshes the near-miss states to match
  the model's current failure modes.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">REVES two-stage loop</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the algorithm</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="step-list">

      <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Run Sequential Revision Rollouts</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            Under the current policy πθ, generate revision trajectories: y₁ ~ πθ(·|x), then
            y_t ~ πθ(·|x, y_&#123;t-1&#125;, feedback(y_&#123;t-1&#125;)) for t=2..K, stopping at first correct response or budget K.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">for x in training_set: rollout = sr_rollout(π, x, K=budget)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Filter: Keep Only Successful Trajectories</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            Discard rollouts that fail entirely (no correct answer within K steps), and skip any that
            succeed on the first try. Retained trajectories have at least one near-miss before success,
            guaranteeing at least one useful recovery target.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">retained = [r for r in rollouts if r.first_correct_step &gt; 1]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Extract: Revision + Verification Prompts</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            From each retained trajectory (x, y₁, …, y_ℓ) with y_ℓ correct, build two prompt types:
            (i) revision prompts asking the model to improve each y_i;
            (ii) verification prompts asking whether y_i is correct.
            The verification prompts train self-stopping — no oracle needed at test time.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">revision_data = [(revise_prompt(x, y_i), y_&#123;i+1&#125;) for y_i in y_1..y_&#123;l-1&#125;]
verify_data  = [(verify_prompt(x, y_i), label_i) for y_i in trajectory]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage II: Single-Turn RL on Augmented Set</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            Train πθ on the union of original RL prompts and the Stage I prompts using standard single-turn RL (GRPO).
            No sequential rollouts inside the RL step — just one (prompt, response) pair per gradient step.
            The updated policy becomes the rollout policy for the next epoch's Stage I.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">augmented = original_prompts + revision_data + verify_data
new_policy = grpo_train(π, augmented)  # single-turn RL only
# → repeat Stage I with new_policy</div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Why single-turn RL is cheaper than multi-turn</h3>
<p>
  Standard multi-turn RL requires online sequential rollouts at every gradient step — the dominant cost
  is the serial K-step revision chain in the inner loop.
  REVES generates augmented data once per epoch in Stage I (which can run <em>in parallel with RL training</em>
  on the previous epoch's data), then trains entirely on single-turn (prompt, response) pairs.
  No per-step sequential rollout is needed during RL training.
</p>

<h3>Verification prompts enable test-time self-stopping</h3>
<p>
  At test time, the oracle stopping signal (ground-truth answer) is usually unavailable.
  REVES trains the model to self-stop using two surrogate signals. For math: the <em>Tail Confidence</em>
  score (token-level confidence averaged over the tail positions of the response — a higher score
  means more concentrated, confident predictions). For code: public test cases as execution feedback.
  The verification prompt training improves AUROC from 72.1% (revision-only) to 74.1% (REVES),
  calibrating the model's self-assessment more accurately.
</p>
</section>

