<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>Why continual augmentation matters:</strong> Augmenting data only at epoch 0 and reusing it
  leads to substantially worse performance than refreshing every epoch. Once the model stops making
  earlier mistakes, stale augmented data is uninformative — the near-miss states no longer reflect
  the model's current failure modes. REVES keeps the supervision aligned with where the model
  currently struggles.
</div>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: REVES — Training LLMs to Recover from Mistakes</h2>
    <p>Standard reinforcement learning trains language models on single-shot rewards — given a question, produce an answer and receive feedback. But at test time, models operate in sequential revision loops: they attempt a problem, get feedback on failure, and try again, repeatedly. This training-deployment gap means that optimizing single-shot performance doesn't necessarily improve multi-step revision capability.</p>
    <p>REVES addresses this by decomposing the sequential revision objective into per-state one-step recovery probabilities. Each wrong intermediate answer in a successful trajectory becomes a standalone revision training task. The algorithm runs in two stages: Stage I generates revision rollouts and extracts near-miss states from successful trajectories; Stage II trains on these augmented prompts using standard single-turn RL. Crucially, augmentation must be refreshed every epoch to track the model's current failure modes.</p>
    <p>Empirically, REVES delivers plus 6.5 points on LiveCodeBench over the RL baseline and plus 4 points over multi-turn RL. On AIME24 with oracle stopping and budget 32, a Qwen2.5-7B gains 12 percentage points over the RL baseline. On the circle packing benchmark, a Qwen3-4B model trained with REVES matches the best results from Gemini-2.0 Pro and Qwen3-8B evolutionary search systems — the smallest model to reach this level. Gains also transfer to MCTS, AB-MCTS, and Mind Evolution, confirming that sequential revision capability improves every revision-using test-time algorithm.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：REVES — 训练大模型从错误中恢复</h2>
    <p>标准强化学习以单次奖励训练语言模型——给定问题，生成答案，获得反馈。但测试时，模型在序列修正循环中运行：尝试问题、根据失败反馈进行修正，反复迭代。这一训练-部署差距意味着优化单次性能不一定能提升多步修正能力。</p>
    <p>REVES通过将序列修正目标分解为逐状态的单步恢复概率来解决这一问题。成功轨迹中每一个错误的中间答案都成为独立的修正训练任务。算法分为两个阶段：第一阶段生成修正轨迹，从成功轨迹中提取"近失"状态；第二阶段使用标准单步强化学习对这些增强提示进行训练。关键在于，每个训练轮次都必须刷新数据增强，以跟踪模型当前的失败模式。</p>
    <p>实验上，REVES在LiveCodeBench上比RL基线提升6.5分，比多步RL提升4分。在预算为32的AIME24测试中，Qwen2.5-7B在Oracle停止条件下比RL基线提升12个百分点。在圆形填充基准测试中，使用REVES训练的Qwen3-4B模型达到了Gemini-2.0 Pro和Qwen3-8B进化搜索系统的最佳结果——以最小的模型达到同等水平。这些收益还延伸至MCTS、AB-MCTS和Mind Evolution，证实序列修正能力可提升所有基于修正的测试时算法。</p>
  </div>
</div>

</section>

